<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Refined Training data</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As our experiments have shown, the model learns to solve easy plans with high probability, while struggling with the harder tasks from the test set.
Since our dataset is biased towards short trajectories, removing these from the training data should help the model generalize better towards the harder tasks.
We can filter the training data for all the plans that move directly towards the target, which we consider easy, and remove them from the training dataset.
We define these plans to have four or fewer key points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.4 Refined Training data ‣ 4 Results ‣ Keypoint-based Diffusion for Robotic Motion Planning on the NICOL Robot" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>c and <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.4 Refined Training data ‣ 4 Results ‣ Keypoint-based Diffusion for Robotic Motion Planning on the NICOL Robot" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>d show the behavior of the models with and without point clouds, trained on the refined dataset.
Reducing the bias in the training data positively affected the model’s performance.
A distribution shift toward the middle can be observed by looking at the distribution of likelihoods.
While some certainty for the easy plans was lost, the model was able to find collision-free solutions for more of the hard scenarios.
It can be seen in Tab. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Results ‣ Keypoint-based Diffusion for Robotic Motion Planning on the NICOL Robot" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> that the model generalizes to the easy trajectories, even though not being trained on them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When we examine the plan length metrics, we see that all models lost a bit of average plan quality by removing the easy samples from the training data.
This lines up with our observations of the distribution of the in-batch success being less concentrated at the extremes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<div id="S4.F6.1" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:82.8pt;"><img src="2509.04076v1/images/hist_50_baseline.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/136;" width="115" height="136" alt="Refer to caption">
